<header><p class="st-kicker">principality · Germany and Central Europe</p><h1 class="st-title">Principality of Blankenburg</h1><p class="st-meta">1707 CE – 1731 CE · capital Blankenburg</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">Harz principality of a Brunswick prince whose daughter became mother of Empress Maria Theresa.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>The County of Blankenburg in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a> had passed to the dukes of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a> in 1599. Duke <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/anthony-ulrich-duke-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Anthony Ulrich</a> gave it to his younger son <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rudolph-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Louis Rudolph</a>, and in 1707 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/joseph-i-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Joseph I</a> raised it to a principality, giving Louis Rudolph a seat among the princes of the Empire.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rudolph-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Louis Rudolph</a> ruled the small Lutheran territory from Blankenburg Castle, which he rebuilt, with a court <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/theatre">theatre</a> and gardens. The principality's prestige came from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/diplomatic-marriage">dynastic marriages</a>: his daughter <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/elisabeth-christine-of-brunswick-wolfenbuttel">Elisabeth Christine</a> married the future <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-vi-holy-roman-emperor">Emperor Charles VI</a> and became mother of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maria-theresa">Maria Theresa</a>, while another daughter married the son of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/peter-the-great">Peter the Great</a>.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>In 1731 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-rudolph-duke-of-brunswick-luneburg">Louis Rudolph</a> inherited the Principality of <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/principality-of-wolfenbuttel">Brunswick-Wolfenbüttel</a>, and Blankenburg was reunited with it. It remained a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brunswick">Brunswick</a> possession and became part of the <a class="enc-link st-link" href="/state/duchy-of-brunswick">Duchy of Brunswick</a>.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/principality-of-blankenburg.jpg" alt="Principality of Blankenburg" width="224" height="168"><figcaption>Image: Gustav Droysen; derivative work by Beckstet · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Blankenburg_1400.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1707 CE – 1731 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/18th-century">18th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>principality</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>feudal</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Blankenburg</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Louis Rudolph</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Lutheranism</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>German</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/germany">Germany</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>County of Blankenburg</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1719&amp;focus=principality-of-blankenburg" aria-label="Principality of Blankenburg on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4927 386 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where Principality of Blankenburg lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M5247 642L5237 632"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M5237 632h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5247 642h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5247 642h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks its recorded location. <a href="/atlas?year=1719&amp;focus=principality-of-blankenburg">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
